<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Acamapichtli</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1376–<a href="/years/1395">1395</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Acamapichtli was the first tlatoani, or ruler, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, reigning c. 1376–1395. He came from a noble lineage connected to Culhuacan, which gave the Mexica dynasty prestige and legitimacy. His reign established the ruling house from which later Aztec emperors descended.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/acamapichtli.jpg" alt="Acamapichtli" width="320" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Acamapichtli,_-_1387,_1395_o_1403,_retrato_an%C3%B3nimo_(~1541).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Acamapichtli, whose name means handful of reeds, was the first tlatoani of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> and the founder of the Aztec ruling dynasty. Chronicles differ on the dates of his reign. He was not a native of Tenochtitlan. After the death of Tenoch, the elders of the Mexica calpultin chose to elect a tlatoani who could secure the new city through ties to powerful groups, and they sent a delegation to the leaders of Culhuacan. Acamapichtli was the child of one of the unions between Culhua and Mexica: his father Opochtli Iztahuatzin was a Mexica leader, and his mother Atotoztli was a daughter of King Coxcoxtli of Culhuacan. The Culhua nobility claimed descent from the Toltecs, which made his lineage prestigious.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Acamapichtli began as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cihuacoatl">cihuacoatl</a>, or governor. He was then 20 and living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a> with his mother, and he entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> with great pomp. He married Ilancueitl, daughter of the ruler of Culhuacan, and took a wife from each Tenochtitlan calpulli to bind the city together. His city fell quickly into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tepanecs">Tepanec</a> orbit and became a tributary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azcapotzalco">Azcapotzalco</a>, paying a tribute said to be oppressive and capricious, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> forces fought for Azcapotzalco against Chalco and other cities before they were allowed to wage war on their own. He avoided trouble with the more powerful ruler Tezozómoc by paying what he demanded; when Tezozómoc asked for a chinampa of flowers, the Mexica raised one and floated it across the lake to him.</p><p>Within the city, Acamapichtli expanded the chinampa system and captured lakeshore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinampa">chinampas</a> from Xochimilco and other cities. The earliest excavated level of the Great Pyramid, Temple II, dates to his reign. The city was divided into four districts, Moyotlán, Zoquipan, Cuecopan and Atzacualco, houses of cane and reeds gave way to stone ones, and the first Aztec laws are said to have been made then.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His first wife bore him no children. Huitzilíhuitl, his son by Tezcatlan Miyahuatzin, succeeded him, and another son, Itzcóatl, who became tlatoani in 1427, was the son of a noble but enslaved woman Acamapichtli had bought in the market of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azcapotzalco">Azcapotzalco</a>. Before his death he asked the chiefs of the four districts to elect his successor, and they chose Huitzilíhuitl. Tlatoani was not strictly a hereditary title, but candidates came from a small class of princes, and all later Aztec rulers descended from Acamapichtli.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Acamapichtli” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Over a century of subordination they built their island into the city of Tenochtitlan, developed the chinampas (raised-bed gardens in the lake) that greatly raised agricultural productivity, established their first royal dynasty under <b>Acamapichtli</b> (r. c. 1375–1395), and earned a reputation for ferocity as mercenary warriors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Ruling houses claimed Toltec descent: the lords of Culhuacan did so, and the Mexica of Tenochtitlan chose as their first king <b>Acamapichtli</b>, who had Culhua ancestry.</blockquote><a href="/empire/toltec-state">Toltec state · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The first Mexica king <b>Acamapichtli</b> had Culhua royal ancestry.</blockquote><a href="/state/culhuacan">Culhuacan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.44,-99.13&amp;year=1385" aria-label="Tenochtitlan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Acamapichtli"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4267 5869h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4267 5869h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.44,-99.13&amp;year=1385">What was here in 1385? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
